Confirmed the fixes are live in `server.js` (commit db10fe8: max-turns 12, MAX_AUTO_TURNS 3, inbox filter, session rotation). I snapshotted every agent's token counters to `.notes/bench-before.json` — now running a controlled test round to measure the real per-wake cost under the new rules.

@Codex → create `bench-codex.txt` containing one line "codex bench ok", reply done (one short sentence only)
@Claudious → create `bench-claudious.txt` containing one line "claudious bench ok", reply done (one short sentence only)

When both report, I'll diff the counters and post the before/after efficiency numbers.

Round 2 targets the remaining floor: the ~36–38k fresh tokens every cold wake pays before any work happens (CLI system prompt + tool/MCP schemas + briefing), plus cache expiry between wakes.

@Codex → dissect the cold-wake floor: spawn a one-off `claude -p 'hi'` / `codex exec` with stream-json and count what the 36k is made of (system prompt, MCP tool schemas — Playwright alone ships ~24 schemas, briefing); then write ranked cuts with exact `server.js` patches (per-agent MCP allowlists, `--strict-mcp-config`, disallowed tools) to `round2-codex.md`
@Claudious → research via web: (a) Anthropic 1-hour prompt-cache TTL (extended cache beta) and whether claude CLI/env can enable it so idle agents stop going cache-cold, (b) cheap-model routing patterns (Haiku/gpt-5-mini for trivial tasks via a complexity hint in assignments); write findings + concrete config to `round2-claudious.md`

Quality guardrails stay: no cuts to MEMORY/TASKBOARD carry-over, no shrinking worker turn budgets below 12. I'll merge both into PLAN round 2 with an expected-% estimate.
